  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Tejpal Singh vs State Of U.P. on 21 November, 2019 Author: Sunita Agarwal Bench: Sunita Agarwal High Court Of Judicature At Allahabad ?Court No. - 41 Case :- Criminal Revision No. - 572 of 1999 Revisionist :- Tejpal Singh Opposite Party :- State of U.P. Counsel for Revisionist :- B.R.Singh Counsel for Opposite Party :- Govt. Advocate Hon'ble Mrs. Sunita Agarwal,J.",
    "facts": "The present revisionist is directed against the judgement and order dated 30.07.1997 passed by the Additional Chief Judicial Magistrate, Mainpuri in Case No. 645 of 1994 under Sections 7/16 of Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, 1954. It appears that a complaint was lodged on 28.09.1988 after receipt of public analyst report regarding sample of milk taken from the possession of the revisionist by the Food Inspector on 29.12.1987. From the perusal of the order impugned, it transpires that sample was sent to the public analyst on 2.01.1988 and report was received on 02.02.1988. After receipt of public analyst report, the Food Tejpal Singh vs State Of U.P. on 21 November, 2019 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/13960054/ 1 Inspector prepared for obtaining sanction to lodge prosecution. In the meantime, the Food Inspector, namely Sri R.N. Saxena who had taken the sample had died. Before the Court below, a witness of the sample taken by the then Food Inspector, namely Sri R.N. Saxena had been produced as P.W.-3 to prove that sample was taken after proper shaking of the milk and all procedures for preservation of milk sample was taken by him. In view of the testimony of P.W.-3, the court below did not find any infirmity in the procedure of obtaining sample and opined that the mandatory provisions of Rules 16 and 17 had been followed by the then Food Inspector. Further, specific objection was taken by the accused regarding non compliance of the provisions of Section 13(2). It was contended that the public analyst report was not provided within requisite period so as to get an independent analysis from the the Central Food Laboratory. As per the public analyst report, milk fat was found deficient to the extent of 6.2%, whereas soluble non fat was deficient to the extent of 7.8%, as a result of which milk was found adulterated, i.e. it was opined water was added to the milk by the accused/revisionist.",
    "arguments": "The Apex Court in the case of Vijendra vs. State of UP reported in AIR 2019 SC 4351 has held that the requirement of Section 13(2) of the Food and Adulteration Act to send the report after institution of prosecution against the person within time prescribed under Rule 9B of Rules, 1955 is mandatory and substantial compliance of the said provision is needed to sustain the conviction. Tejpal Singh vs State Of U.P. on 21 November, 2019 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/13960054/ 2",
    "petitioner_arguments": "",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "The Apex Court in the case of Vijendra vs. State of UP reported in AIR 2019 SC 4351 has held that the requirement of Section 13(2) of the Food and Adulteration Act to send the report after institution of prosecution against the person within time prescribed under Rule 9B of Rules, 1955 is mandatory and substantial compliance of the said provision is needed to sustain the conviction. Tejpal Singh vs State Of U.P. on 21 November, 2019 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/13960054/ 2"
